  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize the function's representation by modal analysis using both frequency-domain and time-domain representations. The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires minimizing the maximum autocorrelation of a step function with fixed integral. Traditional grid-based optimization faces combinatorial complexity with large n_points. Our strategy replaces the grid-based representation with a principled basis decomposition, leveraging the intuition that the optimal function likely exhibits periodic or quasi-periodic properties.\n\nImplementation of this strategy requires:\n- Projecting candidate functions onto harmonic subspaces\n- Using multidomain optimization across frequency bands\n- Maintaining grid consistency through inverse transforms\n- Balancing global exploration with local refinement\n\nThe primary bottleneck addressed is the high dimensionality and non-convexity of the grid optimization space. By decomposing the function into Fourier components, we reduce the search space while preserving essential features. This differs significantly from the previous approach which relies on gradient-based local optimization without explicit harmonic decomposition.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe represent the function h(x) as the inverse Fourier transform of its coefficient vector. For a grid of n_points, the transform is defined as:\n\nh(x_j) = (1/n)^{1/2} * \u2211_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k * e^{2\u03c0i * k * x_j / 2}\n\nConstraints are imposed via:\n\n0 \u2264 Re[h(x)] \u2264 1 for all x\n\u2211_{j=0}^{n-1} h(x_j) * dx = 1\n\nThe objective C5 is the stationary-phase maximum of the correlation, computed via:\n\nC5 = max_k (2/n) * \u2211_j h_j*(g_{j+k})]\nwhere g_j = 1 - h_j\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nThis is a multi-stage optimization strategy:\n\n**Stage 1: Basis initialization**\n- If initial_h_values available, transform to Fourier space\n- Generate orthogonal basis vectors for:\n  - Low-frequency components (domain 0-0.1 cycles)\n  - Mid-frequency components (domain 0.1-0.9 cycles)\n  - High-frequency components (domain 0.9-1 cycles)\n- Apply randomized rounding to projected coefficients (ensuring discrete step function properties)\n\n**Stage 2: Multi-domain coordinate ascent**\n- For each frequency band, apply directed search optimization\n- Low-frequency band: Use generalized pattern search with adaptive radius\n- Mid-frequency band: Use Bayesian optimization with expected improvement\n- High-frequency band: Use sequential quadratic programming with gradient projection\n\n**Stage 3: Wavelet-based projection**\n- Project the optimal frequency components onto wavelet basis to ensure smooth transitions and grid consistency\n- Apply iterative thresholding to remove implausible frequency intersections\n\n**Stage 4: Stepwise refinement**\n- Convert Fourier coefficients to grid space using inverse transform\n- Compute transition matrix between frequency and grid representations\n- Perform local refinement using Nelder-Mead simplex method\n\nAll mathematical operations are performed using optimized SciPy functions with dimension consistency maintained through basis transformations.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nThe core search strategy includes:\n\nPrimary search space decomposition:\n- Low-frequency subspace: 1/6 grid resolution, search radius 0.5 optimum\n- Mid-frequency subspace: 1/3 grid resolution, search radius 1.0 optimum\n- High-frequency subspace: 1/2 grid resolution, search radius 0.8 optimum\n\nThe following variations are included:\n\n(a) Dynamic frequency band expansion/reduction based on gradient flow\n(b) Multi-stage annealing schedule with exponential decay\n(c) Dimension-doubling strategy with monkey-patching projection matrices\n\nEach variation is triggered by monitoring stagnation metrics like gradient norms and will automatically adjust search parameters.\n\n5. Numerical Robustness and Memory Use\nMemory is carefully managed by decomposing the problem into basis vectors and projecting only active components. The implementation:\n\n- Uses cached FP64 matrices for basis transformations\n- Implements dimension-aware basis storage (up to 256\u00d7256)\n- Includes gradual projection from high-frequency components to low-frequency foundation\n- Maintains consistent grid-to-frequency mapping with Jacobian matrices\n\nConvergence is enforced through:\n- Dual-frequency verification (grid and Fourier space check)\n- Gradient clipping at 10^3 magnitudes\n- Dynamic restart when stagnation exceeds 10 iterations\n\n6. Runtime Safeguards and Return Contract\nThe implementation includes:\n\n- Nested timing mechanisms (budget divided between bands)\n- Early termination for underperforming bands\n- Progressive budget allocation based on adversary detection\n- Graceful degradation to simpler methods if numerical instability detected\n\nObject returns will satisfy:\n- np.allclose(h,0.5) ==> fallback to initial_h_values\n- ||\u2207(C5)||_1 > 1e4 ==> switch to modulus adjustment mode\n- Time remaining < 10s ==> fallback to stored best solution\n\nThe Fourier-optimized solution is compared to previous grid optimizations by re-projection into grid space and objective verification. The initial_h_values fallback is prepared from the provided parent solution if Fourier methods fail to converge.",
